   },
   "text": "Threshold Behaviour of a Three-Compartment Epidemic Model"
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i1",
   "kind": "text",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.44739,
    "y": 0.22284,
    "w": 0.10521,
    "h": 0.01509
   },
   "text": "A. Outbreak"
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i2",
   "kind": "text",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.44393,
    "y": 0.25372,
    "w": 0.11215,
    "h": 0.01509
   },
   "text": "October 2026"
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i3",
   "kind": "section_header",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.46419,
    "y": 0.3066,
    "w": 0.07163,
    "h": 0.01258
   },
   "text": "Abstract"
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i4",
   "kind": "text",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.17398,
    "y": 0.3305,
    "w": 0.65205,
    "h": 0.05786
   },
   "text": "We study the classical three-compartment epidemic in fractions: susceptible S, infectious I, and removed R. The total population is conserved, the outbreak grows only above the threshold β/γ > 1, and the infectious fraction peaks when the susceptible pool has crossed below the threshold value."
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i5",
   "kind": "section_header",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.12941,
    "y": 0.41474,
    "w": 0.16036,
    "h": 0.01811
   },
   "text": "1 The model"
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i6",
   "kind": "text",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.12941,
    "y": 0.449,
    "w": 0.45971,
    "h": 0.01377
   },
   "text": "With contact rate β and removal rate γ the dynamics read"
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i7",
   "kind": "formula",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.45662,
    "y": 0.47816,
    "w": 0.41397,
    "h": 0.01556
   },
   "latex": "\\dot { S } = - \\beta S I ,"
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i8",
   "kind": "formula",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.4443,
    "y": 0.50911,
    "w": 0.42629,
    "h": 0.01556
   },
   "latex": "\\dot { I } = \\beta S I - \\gamma I ,"
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i9",
   "kind": "formula",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.46908,
    "y": 0.53439,
    "w": 0.40151,
    "h": 0.01556
   },
   "latex": "\\dot { R } = \\gamma I ."
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i10",
   "kind": "section_header",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.12941,
    "y": 0.5761,
    "w": 0.35908,
    "h": 0.01811
   },
   "text": "2 Initial state and parameters"
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i11",
   "kind": "text",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.12941,
    "y": 0.61036,
    "w": 0.74119,
    "h": 0.04799
   },
   "text": "The cohort starts almost fully susceptible, with the field-recorded initial state S(0) = 0.99, I(0) = 0.01 and R(0) = 0. The nominal parameters are β = 0.4 and γ = 0.1; at these values the threshold quantity β/γ exceeds one and an outbreak follows."
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i12",
   "kind": "section_header",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.12941,
    "y": 0.6845,
    "w": 0.15696,
    "h": 0.01811
   },
   "text": "3 Discussion"
  },
  {
   "id": "p1-i13",
   "kind": "text",
   "page": 1,
   "bbox": {
    "x": 0.12941,
    "y": 0.71876,
    "w": 0.74117,
    "h": 0.04799
   },
   "text": "Because removal never returns to the susceptible pool, the sum S + I +R stays constant along every trajectory; the outbreak burns out by depleting the susceptible pool, leaving a final susceptible fraction well below its initial value."
  }
 ]
}